From febf9afdbc66a8ce9c4092923bce24d7335fbd35 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 00:02:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][Settings] The file cache: 2 GB from 28 GiB of RAM,
 and the <cache> of advancedsettings.xml taken again

Since Kodi 21 the file cache is set in Settings > Services > Caching, and Kodi reads nothing of the <cache> of
advancedsettings.xml. Guides and forum answers still give it (forum thread 30362, #813: a
<memorysize> of 268435456 for a FEL remux streamed over HTTP), and it is left out without a word in kodi.log. Its
values are now taken as the defaults of those settings, in the units Kodi up to 20 had them (memorysize in bytes, 0
for the cache on disk; buffermode by the same codes; readfactor as a factor, 4.0; chunksize in bytes), each brought
to the nearest choice of its setting that is not more. A value set in the settings still wins, and kodi.log says
which one is used; a text that is no value of the setting is said and left out. The memory is taken up to a quarter
of the RAM (Kodi allocates all of it while a file plays) and 2 GB, the largest choice, which CFileCache allows.

The default from the RAM (since 1.0: 64 MB, 256 MB from 3.5 GiB, 512 MB from 7 GiB, 1 GB from 14 GiB) gains a step:
2 GB from 28 GiB, the same 1/14 of the RAM. A Matroska file holds 3/8 of the setting ahead of what plays (two
buffers of half the size, each 3/4 ahead: CFileCache::Open()); at the 130 Mbit/s of the densest Dolby Vision FEL
remuxes that is 12 s with 512 MB, 25 s with 1 GB and 50 s with 2 GB, so a server that stalls for half a minute is
ridden out on a PC that has the memory.

The rule and the conversions are in FileCacheDefaults.h, one place for the default and for advancedsettings.xml,
with the lists of choices of the settings (a host test compares them with the fillers of ServicesSettings.cpp).
